191: Chapter 190\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
{\b
I think I'll address something here. I keep getting a lot of complaints about this story. Well, there's always whiny complainers over every story, especially because my mc's aren't the ching chong slop edgy losers that hide every little facet of their personalities and abilities and have less personality than Plank from Ed Edd n' Eddy.}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
{\b
Specifically though, I want to address the complaints over Ash Ketchum in this story, and how well he's doing.}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
{\b
First, I'll go over, ugh, the scaling of pokemon series. As a rule, I hate doing this shit, cuz power scaling is such virgin behaviour, and I know a bunch of Red fanboys are going to seethe and cry because their precious silent, red-eyed dot dot dot self insert won't be made out as powerful as they want. And there are a lot of you consistently wanking off Red in the comments of this story. Seriously, wash Red's cum from your hands before you type these comments please.}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
{\b
Anyway, there's a hierarchy among the pokemon series strength wise. Of the main series, Power wise, the most powerful pokemon series, is the main anime, followed by the Pokemon Adventure/Specials manga, Then Pokemon Electric Tales of Pikachu, then Pokemon Horizons, then Pokemon Origins and the Games are about on equal level give or take.}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
{\b
To be frank, Ash and Red from the Adventures manga, by time they get their 8th badges respectively, could solo all of Horizons, Origins and and the games without much issue at all. That's how big of a gulf there are, and at those points Ash and Manga Red are far from their peaks.}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
{\b
Now putting that side, let's focus on the pokemon leagues of the anime. In the Adventures Manga, the league isn't even properly made until much later in the series, there isn't even an a proper League Elite Four and Champion when Red gets his badges and it's just an open tournament anyone can compete in. It isn't until post Hoenn if I remember right, proper leagues are established, and it's just the same as the game ones.}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
{\b
Now the anime. It's very different. Putting aside the eight badges, The pokemon leagues are yearly things in each region, at different times for each. Where the best of the best turn up. Anyone of these guys could Solo Origins, Horizons or the games. And to become a champion in the anime, you need to go through a tournament full of these guys. Only when you win this, do you get the chance to take on the Elite Four and then possibly the Champion.}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
{\b
Easier said than done however. The Elite Four, are on a completely different level from normal trainers. Let's just use Flint as an example from Sinnoh. When Ash fought him, it beat Pikachu, Infernape and Buizel without issue. It flat out took Pikachu's Volt Tackle to the chest, and didn't even flinch or move back. This same Pikachu beat Regice remember, commanded by Brandon at that.}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
{\b
If an 8th Gym Leader was say, Saiyan Saga Vegeta level, an Elite Four member would be more akin to first form Frieza from the Saiyan Saga. The gulf is truly that massive. Well, before Sun and Moon Journeys nerfed the fuck out of them. And a champions ace like Cynthia, could outright while immobilzed, face tank the Frenzy Plant of a Torterra at very least, on the 8th gym leader tier of power.}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
{\b
Now, why is this important. Frankly, despite his sheer idiocy at times, and the writer leaks outright admitting they refused to let Ash win and gave him anti-plot armour. Ash was without a doubt, a prodigy.}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
{\b
It's established very early in the anime, that most trainers can ever hope to win a few badges, never mind to make it to the leagues consistently. Just liken Pokemon Battling, to sports of our world, like Football, Basketball etc. Your run of the mill joe ain't gonna go far there. And it ain't like the leagues only have people from one region, no, these people come from all over the world for each league. The best of the best from the entire world turn up at these leagues. And Ash consistently, from his very league, scored incredibly high. Just his first three were top 16, top 8 and top 8 again. And everyone knows, if Ash used his older pokemon in the Hoenn League, he would have won. So at that point, in canon, where he would have been 13 at most, he was already good enough to win a league.}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
{\b
But putting aside main Ash's abilities for a moment. I want to focus more, on the fact that early on, Ash was very catch crazy. He wanted to catch every pokemon he saw. He just, didn't get the chance because of literally, Team Rocket, Misty and Brock always getting in his way. Oh and Samurai that one time.}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
{\b
Seriously, Brock and Misty were horrible fucking friends early on in the series.}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
{\b
Anyway why is this important? Because this was never just a pokemon anime story. It's a fusion of the Pokemon Anime, Electric Tale of Pikachu, and Pokemon I Choose You anime AU-verse. Why is this important? Because the characters are a fusion as well. So Brock and Misty are a lot more mature here.}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
{\b
And this goes double for Ash, if I said main Anime Ash was was a prodigy, then I Choose You Ash is like, a super prodigy or something. That Ash caught only a few pokemon first starting out - Pikachu, Butterfree and Charizard. By time he had four badges, he had a fully evolved Charizard. Not only that, but Pikachu went on to 1v1 Marshadow, then right after, fought Ho-oh. And it's implied at the end, that Pikachu actually won that fight. Even if he didn't though. Four badges in, and this Ash is already capable of raising pokemon to fight high-tier legends like Ho-oh.}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
{\b
So the Ash of this story, is a fusion of his anime self that is much more catch happy with nobody to get in his way this time, the prodigal ability of I Choose You Ash, and the maturity of Electric Tales of Pikachu Ash. And a reminder that, Electric Tales of Pikachu Ash, was literally banging Misty and sent a letter telling his mother all about it, even how he and Misty were banging in a bath. And that's just after the Orange League, when he's about 12. In Electric Tales, he travelled the Orange Islands with Misty, Gary and Ritchie. And even caught an enormous alpha slowpoke, to trade to Gary for a picture of his sister and advice on her. Yes, not only was he banging Misty, Electric Tales Ash wanted to bang Gary's sister as well.}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
{\b
God this drew on way longer than I intended. But yes, that's why the Ash of this story, is doing so well.}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
{\b
Now I'm sure some of you will be thinking. But wait Big Papa Ding Dong Jord, how come Jord could hurt Lance's pokemon then and take two down if Champions are such a big deal. Well, putting aside Poke-Jords pokemon are generally a lot bigger, stronger and more durable than they usually would be at their level because of the intense gravity training they do. Lance and his pokemon were taking it easy on him. Because it was being televised, Lance didn't want to absolutely demolish an up and coming rookie with such promise. And he wanted to see just what all Poke-Jords pokemon were capable of, and if there was anything he could take from it.. If he went full force, none of Poke-Jords pokemon would have gotten much of any hits in, if any.}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
{\b
Keep in mind, Lance and the other champions, really are like Shonen Mc's of their own story, that already completed their main story.}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
{\b
You'll understand in a few chapters I guess.}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
{\b
...And yeah, sorry about the yapping that went on way longer than I intended.}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
